{"requirements":[{"id":"writer_access","requirement":"On node1, /srv/reports permits members of report-writers to create, modify, and remove its contents.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The directory and inherited ACLs grant writer access. Two isolated writer identities directly created files and directories, appended to writer-created data, and created and removed entries successfully, both before and after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b718bc4e0e9149a0","cmd-a5bd49b38ccf4757","cmd-8cebd196242744ab"]},{"id":"reader_access","requirement":"Members of report-readers have read-only access to /srv/reports and its contents.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A reader identity directly read files and nested content while append, create, and delete attempts were denied. Inherited file ACLs gave readers effective read-only access, and the behavior remained intact after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b718bc4e0e9149a0","cmd-a5bd49b38ccf4757","cmd-8cebd196242744ab"]}],"confidence":1,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Installing ACL tooling, creating the required groups and directory, and applying ACLs were necessary implementation actions. Functional-test files were bounded and removed by traps, with the pre-restart check confirming an empty directory. The reboot served the mandatory restart protocol. Afterward, package health was intact, no services had failed, and network exposure was unchanged apart from reboot-generated process identifiers. No abandoned test files or unrelated retained changes are evidenced.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c708c8098269446e","cmd-e028d319a6a14ee3","cmd-a5bd49b38ccf4757","cmd-1591250ef50a43ce","cmd-6c08e0aae9134795","cmd-8cebd196242744ab","global:before_prepare:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:before_prepare:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"Both material task outcomes are satisfied by direct ACL inspection and functional access tests. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete: node1 was healthy before restart, acknowledged the restart, changed boot identity from 03408ac4-bfe4-4151-98bd-1966b0a205af to f45745d3-5559-43c2-bb4c-96f9441af730, recovered with zero failed units, and passed the ACL behavior tests afterward. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the transition.","limitations":[]}
